---
<Page
  title="abap2UI5 · for decision makers"
  description="abap2UI5 for decision makers - what you can build with it, why it beats every other way to build UI5 apps on SAP, what your organization keeps, what it costs (nothing) and how to start with one real screen."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div>
        <h1>Modern UI5 apps, built by <em>the team you already have</em></h1>
        <p class="lede">abap2UI5 turns your ABAP developers into UI5 app builders - <b>no JavaScript, no extra platform, no license fee.</b> Faster to build, cheaper to run and freer to leave than any other UI5 solution on the market.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href={PLAYGROUND}>See it live</a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#when-to-use-it-and-when-not">Compare it</a>
        </div>
        <p class="note">Nothing to install, no trial license, no sales call — the playground runs in your browser.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="window">
        <div class="window-bar" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>an abap2UI5 app · one ABAP class</span></div>
        <img src={`${base}walkthrough-preview.webp`} alt="An abap2UI5 app: a selection form above a table of invoices, with a dialog editing one row" width="1000" height="598" fetchpriority="high">
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Stats />

  <section class="section" id="the-problem-it-answers" aria-labelledby="h-problem">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">What you can build</p>
      <h2 id="h-problem">One framework for every process that needs a screen</h2>
      <p class="intro">From the warehouse floor to the management cockpit: whatever runs in SAP today on paper, in Excel or in SAP GUI becomes a modern app - built in your own system, by your own team.</p>
      <div class="cards four">
        {useCases.map(([icon, h, t, cls, alt]) => (
          <div class="card shot">
            <a class="shot-img" href={`${PLAYGROUND}samples/${cls}/`}><img src={`${base}apps/${cls}.webp`} alt={alt} width="800" height="500" loading="lazy"></a>
            <Icon name={icon} /><h3>{h}</h3><p>{t}</p>
            <a class="shot-link" href={`${PLAYGROUND}samples/${cls}/`}>See the example app</a>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="more">→ Browse the <a href={`${PLAYGROUND}samples/`}>sample catalog</a> for the screen closest to yours, or read <a href={`${DOCS}get_started/about`}>In a Nutshell</a> for how it works.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt" id="when-to-use-it-and-when-not" aria-labelledby="h-fit">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Compared with the market</p>
      <h2 id="h-fit">Better than every other way to build UI5 apps on SAP</h2>
      <p class="intro">SAP's own routes, and the UI5 app platforms and low-code builders sold on top of SAP, side by side.</p>
      <div class="table compare">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col" class="us">abap2UI5</th>{rivals.map((r) => <th scope="col">{r}</th>)}</tr></thead>
          <tbody>{compare.map(([k, a, ...rest]) => <tr><th scope="row">{k}</th><td class="us">{a}</td>{rest.map((t) => <td>{t}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="fit">
        <div class="yes">
          <h3>Why teams choose abap2UI5</h3>
          <ul class="reasons">{useIt.map(([b, t]) => <li><b>{b}</b>{t}</li>)}</ul>
        </div>
        <div class="yes">
          <h3>Nothing to throw away</h3>
          <ul class="reasons">
            <li><b>Your Fiori apps stay.</b>abap2UI5 apps appear as tiles in the same launchpad, with navigation between them.</li>
            <li><b>Your ABAP logic stays.</b>A report, a BAPI, a function module: an app calls it as it is.</li>
            <li><b>Your rules stay.</b>Transports, code review, ATC and authorizations apply unchanged.</li>
            <li><b>Your exit stays open.</b>No contract, no runtime fee - the apps are yours whatever you decide later.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="more">→ <a href={`${DOCS}advanced/insights/30-on-stack-or-side-by-side`}>On-Stack or Side-by-Side</a> and <a href={`${DOCS}advanced/insights/31-one-app-many-systems`}>One App, Many Systems</a> are the deployment scenarios in detail.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="ai" aria-labelledby="h-ai">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">AI</p>
      <h2 id="h-ai">The UI5 framework made for AI</h2>
      <p class="intro">An AI assistant is only as good as what it has to produce. With abap2UI5 that is one ABAP class - the simplest target there is for an AI, and the easiest result for your team to check. Your developers describe the screen; the AI writes it; your rules review and transport it like any other code.</p>
      <div class="ai-split">
        <a class="shot-img ai-shot" href={`${PLAYGROUND}samples/z2ui5_cl_smp_app_540/`}><img src={`${base}apps/z2ui5_cl_smp_app_540.webp`} alt="A chat assistant in an abap2UI5 app, with suggested questions" width="800" height="500" loading="lazy"></a>
        <p>Example: <a href={`${PLAYGROUND}samples/z2ui5_cl_smp_app_540/`}>a chat assistant</a> inside an abap2UI5 app - one class, with a built-in rule-based answerer that one method turns into a real AI call.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cards three">
        {ai.map(([icon, h, t]) => <div class="card"><Icon name={icon} /><h3>{h}</h3><p>{t}</p></div>)}
      </div>
      <p class="callout"><b>No AI lock-in either.</b> Use Claude, ChatGPT, Copilot or a local model - with your own key, at your own price. Commercial platforms sell their AI as an add-on to their license; abap2UI5's costs nothing.</p>
      <p class="more">→ Try the <a href={`${PLAYGROUND}ai/`}>AI Studio</a>, or read <a href={`${DOCS}get_started/ai`}>Developing with AI</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt" id="what-you-keep" aria-labelledby="h-keep">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">What you keep</p>
      <h2 id="h-keep">An abap2UI5 app is ABAP code, and governs itself like it</h2>
      <p class="intro">The decision everything else follows from: one ABAP class per app, no generated artifacts, no tables that describe a screen.</p>
      <div class="table">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col" class="us">abap2UI5</th><th scope="col">A tool that stores apps as configuration</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{keep.map(([k, a, b]) => <tr><th scope="row">{k}</th><td class="us">{a}</td><td>{b}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="intro">So abap2UI5 needs no rule of its own. Every rule that already applies to ABAP — naming conventions, code review, ATC, the four-eyes principle on transports — applies to an app without any change. A system closed for repository changes is closed for abap2UI5 apps.</p>
      <div class="cards five">
        {governance.map(([icon, h, t]) => <div class="card"><Icon name={icon} /><h3>{h}</h3><p>{t}</p></div>)}
      </div>
      <p class="callout"><b>No shadow IT.</b> There is no click-together designer that bypasses your development process: every app stays one kind of thing, a class, governed by the rules you already have.</p>
      <p class="more">→ More on <a href={`${DOCS}configuration/security`}>Security</a> and <a href={`${DOCS}configuration/authorization`}>Authorization</a>, and the <a href={`${GITHUB}/blob/main/docs/security.md`}>security model</a> a security review starts from: what the framework guarantees, and which test holds the code to each guarantee.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section start" id="no-strings-attached" aria-labelledby="h-strings">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">No strings attached</p>
      <h2 id="h-strings">Nothing to buy, nothing to renew, nothing between you and your code</h2>
      <p class="intro">What a commercial platform cannot offer, and what stays true after the decision.</p>
